('026b6539-0a22-5558-95ab-eb5bb3c5a64b'::uuid,'est','"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"'::jsonb,'hard','mcq','"Given: △XYZ and XZ ∥ YR. Prove: m∠1 + m∠2 + m∠3 = 180°.\n[Figure: Triangle XYZ with Z at left, Y at right on line ZYT extended to T; R up-right such that YR ∥ XZ; ∠1 at Z, ∠2 at X, ∠3 = ∠XYT interior at Y, ∠4 between XY and YR, ∠5 = ∠RYT.]\nProof: 1. XZ ∥ YR (Given); 2. ∠1 ≅ ∠5 (?); 3. ∠2 ≅ ∠4 (Alternate interior angles of parallel lines are congruent); 4. m∠3 + m∠4 + m∠5 = 180° (∠ZYT is a straight angle); 5. m∠1 + m∠2 + m∠3 = 180° (Substitution)\nWhat is the reason in Step 2?"'::jsonb,'[{"key":"A","text":"Vertical angles are congruent."},{"key":"B","text":"Complementary angles are congruent."},{"key":"C","text":"Corresponding angles of parallel lines are congruent."},{"key":"D","text":"Alternate interior angles of parallel lines are congruent."}]'::jsonb,'{"source":"Kimi Agent Math PDF Question Bank (4)","source_file":"book_t10.md","source_sha256":"f20516078371096927f6322b82ff1576ab3861b0fb64e776ba5ff7fbc66dbac0","source_question_id":"T10-44","import_batch":"est2-kimi-bank-2026-09-21","curriculum_lesson":"Triangles, Trigonometry & Similarity"}'::jsonb,'"C"'::jsonb,'"Source-keyed answer from book_t10.md (T10-44)."'::jsonb),
